  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a4da58d2dc2409c459ef20449bf92a90, type: 3}
  m_Name: Truss
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lessonId: What is Truss
  lessonTitle: What is Truss
  lessonImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c7ad14becd77b714a820300c6ed3a180, type: 3}
  lessonDescription: 'If you have ever seen a bridge that looks like it is made out
    of a giant metal web or scaffolding, you are looking at a Truss bridge!


    Unlike
    a simple beam bridge, a truss structure uses a framework of straight pieces of
    metal or wood connected at joints to form a series of interconnected triangles.


    The
    Engineering Inside:

    Why triangles? In engineering, the triangle is the strongest
    geometric shape. If you push down on a square, it will easily collapse into a
    diamond. But if you push down on the top point of a triangle, the weight is evenly
    distributed down its two sides, while the bottom side stretches to hold it all
    together.


    When a Live Load (like a vehicle) drives across a truss, the
    heavy weight is instantly spread out across the entire framework. Some pieces
    of the triangle will be squeezed together (Compression), while other pieces are
    pulled apart (Tension).


    The Challenge:

    Remember how Beam Bridges bend
    and snap if the gap is too long? Truss bridges solve this problem! Because the
    triangle framework is so incredibly rigid, a truss can safely span much longer
    distances than a beam bridge without needing any support piers placed in the
    water below. It is also surprisingly lightweight because it uses a skeletal frame
    rather than solid, heavy blocks of material.


    Engineer''s Tip: If your bridge
    is sagging or snapping under pressure, look at its shape. Are your bars forming
    squares? Add diagonal bars to turn those weak squares into strong triangles!'
